---
description: A PR does not go out with work it names as not done, or a figure it cannot explain
alwaysApply: true
---

# No open ends in a PR

A PR is opened when its work is finished, not when it runs. These sentences do not belong in a PR body or in a report to the owner:

- "Not measured." A PR that adds a step on a measured path (license verification, server startup, checkout and renewal, binary size) measures it before it is opened, tunes what stands out, and reports the numbers before and after under the PR's `## Measurements`. What is left is stated with a number and the reason it stays.
- "We take it that ..., not checked." An explanation that one look can check is checked before it is written. Code or a design statement that says it works as a dependency does names the source it was compared with: the package's commit (`repository commit` in its `.nuspec`) and the file, and each difference found. In PR #417 a comment said that `ServiceStore.InWriteTransactionAsync` runs as AuraORM's `InTransactionAsync` except for the commit's token; read at the commit of AuraORM 0.1.0, AuraORM also skips the rollback when the body completed the transaction itself, which `ServiceStore` never does.
- "The cause is not confirmed." A figure or a failure the agent cannot explain is traced to its cause from the data before the PR is opened. A guess is not written as an explanation, with or without "probably".

What is out of the row's scope is a roadmap row that names when (`.cursor/rules/defer.mdc`), not a caveat. A finding about code the row did not change becomes its own Issue at once (`.cursor/rules/pace.mdc`).

A reason written as likely ("probably", 「と考えられる」) is checked first when a cheap check exists.

- A figure that moves after a change is traced by measuring again with that one change switched off. In M4a1 an audited write fell from 3.1–3.5 ms to 1.1 ms after the data layer moved to a query builder; with SQLite's WAL mode switched off the same build took 3.7–5.0 ms, so the cause was WAL, not the builder (PR #47).
- A measurement that needs what the local machine lacks (a PostgreSQL server, another OS) is taken on CI, in a step that prints the value to the job summary. In M4a1 the first PR body said the start time on PostgreSQL was not measured, and the review asked for it (PR #47). A measurement that no existing job takes runs in a temporary workflow added to the PR: its `pull_request` trigger names its own file under `paths`, its script runs with `set +e` (or prints each command's exit code) so that the first non-zero code does not end it, and the file is removed before the PR is ready, with the run's number in the PR body. In V1c3c the macOS quarantine (run 42361806393) and the step runner's path checks on Linux and macOS (run 42362464278) were measured this way; the first run stopped at `spctl`'s exit code under bash's `-e` (PR #237). `set +e` does not change that a step ends with the exit code of its last command: end each measuring step with `exit 0`. In V1c4a the version step listed a file that was not there, its last `ls` exited with 1, and the check step after it was skipped (PR #249).
- A value from a GitHub-hosted runner is written as the range of more than one run, with the reason: each run gets another machine. In M4a1 two runs of the same step gave 437–463 ms and 270–291 ms (PR #47). The time of a job or of a step from the jobs API is such a value. In V1c5 the first PR body compared one run of the site job with one run of the PR before it; the site job then took 304 s on one head and 257 s on the next, and its build step, which the commit between them did not touch, took 131 s and 109 s (PR #304).
- A rule that leaves runs out of a judgment states, when it is proposed, what the left-out runs can hide, and that limit goes to the owner to accept. V1d31 judges the activation p99 only on jobs whose disk flush p99 is at most 20 ms; the flush check writes to the same disk as the product, so a regression that shows only on a slow disk is not judged. The independent review found it (M1), and the owner accepted it as a written limit (PR #428).
- A latency tail that misses its target is traced in the same run: record the time of the suspected resource beside the slow requests and compare their timestamps. A suspected cause is named only after its source is read. In V1b the activation p99 missed in two of three jobs; SQLite's busy wait (`HAVE_USLEEP`) was suspected first, but the vendored SQLite sleeps with `nanosleep` by default. A run that flushed 4 KiB to the same disk and waited 50 ms, over and over, showed the two slow activations at the moment one flush took 64 ms (PR #183).
- A conclusion drawn from a measurement goes no further than what the measurement can tell apart, and a derived figure (a ratio, a value per item) is computed by a script and written with its formula. In M6a the resident memory around 4,000 C threads was said to show that each thread's message array is collected, while an array left behind would have grown it by about the same amount; a ratio worked out by hand said "at most 3%" where it was 6.7% (PR #138).
- A behavior measured on one OS is written with that OS, and a claim about every OS is measured on each. In V1c10 the Grill measured on Windows that `Path.GetFullPath("   ")` throws "The path is empty." and the design stated it without the OS; on Linux and macOS a path of spaces is a valid relative path, and the correctness review (A4) found it. .NET's path handling differs by OS (`Path.Windows.cs` and `Path.Unix.cs`) (PR #241).
- A value compared with an earlier build names that build (its commit). In M4a2 two sizes of the M4a1 build stood in the design, 2,048 bytes apart, and neither said which build it was (PR #55).
- Two figures are compared only when they measure the same thing, and each names what it measured (which program, built how). A difference between figures of different things is not explained by a cause that was not measured. In PR #265 the size of the Tutorial 1 app that the first-time reader published (3.4 MB) was set beside a Hello World published on another machine (1.1 MB), and the difference was put down to the Visual Studio version, which nobody had measured; the Issue #264 comment was corrected.
- A difference in the time of a step that runs many parts (the Guide steps step runs every page) is split per part before it is put on the change. In V1c4f the Windows Guide steps took 79 s longer than in PR #293's run; the new page took 2.2 s, and most of the rest was pages the PR did not touch, the NativeAOT publish page going from 17 s to 55 s. A page's time was read from the log, from its first line to the next page's first line (PR #297).
- `release.yml` runs on pull requests and tags, not on pushes to `main`, so a time of `release.yml` is compared with the run of the latest pull request that does not hold the change, and the PR names both runs. PR #281 compared its run with the run of PR #277's head.
- Binary sizes of two runs are compared only after the SDK and runtime versions of both runs are read in their logs (`dotnet-install` prints them). In PR #307 the sizes on Windows and macOS grew by 16 to 1,536 bytes between two runs with no change to compiled code: the runner's SDK had gone from 10.0.403 to 10.0.404. Until `main` has a run with the same SDK, the PR says that the two are not compared.

A figure of a step taken off a measured path stays in the design as the result before and after (16 ms → 7 ms). Its breakdown may stay in the PR body, as measured facts; a cause that was not found is not written in either. If the cause matters, it becomes a roadmap row. In M4b2 the design said the cause of 8 ms was "not known", and two reviews in a row asked for it to go (PR #67).

A commit, a PR, an Issue number, or a figure named in a report to the owner is copied from the output of the command that shows it, not written from memory. In M4b2 a report on PR #67 named a commit hash that did not exist, and the correction came two reports later (PR #71's retrospective). A hash that goes into a PR body or a comment is read from `git log --oneline -1` (or `git rev-parse --short`) in the same step that writes the text. In M4d3 a PR body named `50b8f1e` for the commit `11e6240` (PR #91). In M5c a review summary on Issue #116 named `f9b1a9d` for `100b907`, written before the commit's output was read (PR #118's retrospective).

Figures that are compared or added up come from the same run. In M4c3 the machine's load changed between two runs and every figure of the second was about 1.8 times the first; parts taken from both did not add up to the total (PR #79).

A figure kept in the design is measured on the code of the PR's head. A change to the code after the measurement means measuring again, and the heading names the commit of the code measured. In M4d2 two fixes went in after the measurement and the PR said the figures were not taken again; the review asked for them (PR #87). A fix for a review counts: in M6b the fix of `elm_activation_destroy` made `elasticlm.dll` 1,024 bytes larger, the PR body said so, and the design kept the figures of the head before it until the lessons PR (PR #142). A merge of `origin/main` that brings in only the changes of other PRs, each already measured, is not such a change: `Measure` is not run again, and the PR body names the head each value was taken on. PR #382 kept its values of da51078 after `origin/main` was merged in, and PR #391 those of 1c07553. The same holds for the values in the PR body's `## Measurements`: in PR #400 the review's fix changed `elm` and `BoundedAnswer` after `Measure` ran on 691429c, the run on the fixed head a63f7ac was started only when the owner's review asked for the body, and it ended after the merge, so its values went to a comment.

A measurement is compared with the baseline the Issue names. Another baseline is used only after the owner changes the Issue; otherwise the PR says `Part of #N`, not `Closes #N`. In M4d1 the Issue named `elm-server`, the PR compared with the service's own activation and closed the Issue, and the review caught it (PR #83).

A figure of an earlier row that the Issue names for comparison is measured again with that figure's own method (the same harness and the same kind of build) on the head, and the two are put side by side. A figure taken another way is not compared with it. In M4d3 the Issue named the 6.7–7.5 µs of M3c's NativeAOT self-test, the PR set a JIT test of a longer line beside it, and the review asked for the comparison (PR #91).

The two sides of a timed comparison are measured the same way: both on warm connections or both on new ones, and both with or without the client's own checks. The order of the batches is swapped once to see whether the first batch of a round is slower. In M4d1 most of a gap between acquiring a lease and activating came from the batch order and a new connection per device, not from the request (PR #83).

A comment in the code is held to the same line: it says what was measured, and marks a cause that was not confirmed as not confirmed, with the Issue number.

```text
# BAD
トークン検証の速度は詰めていません。初回だけ遅いですが、おそらく JIT です。

# GOOD
トークン検証は NativeAOT で 1 件 41 µs → 18 µs。内訳を測ると公開鍵の ImportSubjectPublicKeyInfo が 6 割だったので、
鍵 ID ごとに ECDsa を作って使い回すようにした。初回の 2.1 ms は ECDsa の生成で、JIT ではない（JitInfo で確認）。
```
